# The job-boundary growth guard (tests/check-vm-growth.sh) runs every
# tests/*.ps as a Control-D-framed job in one --jobserver process and holds
# both banks of virtual memory to the baseline after each. A workload that
# cannot be a job in such a stream -- one that never reaches its Control-D --
# is named here, with the reason, and left out of the stream. The population
# is otherwise the whole directory; only the exceptions are listed, so a new
# test is held to the baseline the day it lands.
#
#   <workload>  <why it cannot be a job in a stream>

interrupt_test.ps   an infinite loop with no exit: it never reaches its Control-D, so it would stall the stream. Its own harness (run-interrupt-test.sh) breaks the loop with an external interrupt this guard does not send.

# ---- workloads that end their own encapsulation ----
#
# exitserver makes the job unencapsulated (PLRM 3.7.7), and what such a job
# does to virtual memory afterwards is permanent by definition: that is the
# whole of what an administrator job is for. The reading taken after one is
# therefore a new baseline rather than a leak, and every job after it would be
# reported against the first reading if it were not.
#
# These lines are held to the source in both directions: the check derives the
# set by reading each workload for exitserver outside a comment, so a test that
# stops ending its encapsulation stops being excused on the same day, and a
# test that starts is reported until it is named here.
#
#   <workload>  administrator  <what it is an administrator job for>

cacheparams_range_test.ps  administrator  the font cache size may be stated only in an administrator job (PLRM 8.2, setcacheparams), so the cases that state one run past exitserver
op_fontcache_test.ps       administrator  likewise: the refusal an ordinary job meets is measured first, then the same request is made again as an administrator
semantics_test.ps          administrator  it asks what exitserver itself does -- that the job continues, that the operand stack is cleared and that the begun serverdict is unwound
